#{\footnote keyboard}${\footnote The Keyboard, Bend and Mod Wheel}+{\footnote main:040}K{\footnote keyboard;keys;Hold;latch;Keys;computer keyboard;pitch bend;Bend;mod wheel;Mod;octave}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b The Keyboard, Bend and Mod Wheel\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Along the bottom of the synth is a keyboard made of real Windows push buttons (five octaves, C2 to C7).\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To play the keyboard\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Click a key to play it. Hold the mouse button down to hold the note.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Drag across the keys to play one note after another.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Notes from your DAW push their keys down, so you can watch what plays.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To hold notes\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Click {\b Hold}. Notes keep sounding after you let go.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Click {\b Hold} again to release them.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To play from the computer keyboard\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 1\tab Click {\b Keys}.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 2\tab Type on the middle row: {\f1 a w s e d f t g y h u j k} play C to C, like the white and black keys.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 3\tab Press {\f1 z} for an octave down and {\f1 x} for an octave up.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Bend and Mod\par
\pard\sa30\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Bend\tab The pitch bend. It springs back to the middle when you let go.\par
\pard\sa30\li1555\ri175\fi-1440\tx1555\plain\f0\fs20 Mod\tab The mod wheel. It stays where you leave it. It adds to Macro 1.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 When your DAW sends pitch bend or mod wheel, these controls follow it.\par
\pard\sb40\sa60\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b Note:} Notes you play on this keyboard go through the plugin, just like notes from your DAW. To record them, record the plugin's output.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb Macros and the Mod Wheel}{\v macros}\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb Playing the Instrument}{\v playing}\par
\page
#{\footnote strips}${\footnote The Strips}+{\footnote main:050}K{\footnote strips;recorders;Play;Stop;wave;position;length}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b The Strips\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Each strip is one real Sound Recorder, without its menu bar and title bar. There are sixteen strips.\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 Status\tab What the recorder is doing: Stopped, Playing, or the command it is carrying out while a sound is built.\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 Position\tab The time of the play position, in seconds.\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 Wave\tab The green wave display. It follows the sound.\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 Length\tab The length of the sound, in seconds.\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 Seek\tab Shows and sets the position.\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 Rewind, Forward\tab Move to the start or the end.\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 Play, Stop\tab Play or stop this recorder.\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 Record\tab Sound Recorder's own Record button. The instrument does not use it.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A note can sound more than one recorder at once: {\ul layers}{\v term_layer}, {\ul harmony}{\v term_harmony} and {\ul unison}{\v term_unison} all add recorders. When a note needs a recorder and none is free, the oldest one stops quickly and plays the new note.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Watching a sound being built\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 While Windows builds a sound, the recorder's row number lights up, and its Status field names each command as it runs (Open, Echo, Insert ding, Seek 500 ms).\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The old sound keeps playing until the new one is ready, so nothing you play is lost.\par
\page
#{\footnote presets}${\footnote Presets}+{\footnote main:060}K{\footnote presets;programs;factory presets;categories;user presets;copying a sound}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Presets\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 BASILISK 16 has 71 factory presets, named "Category: Name". They are in the preset list of your DAW and in the Preset list at the top of the synth. Click < or > to step through them.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Categories: Keys, Bells, Pads, Bass, Plucks, Percussion, Collage, FX, Macro, Brass and Library.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Some of the factory presets\par
\pard\sa30\li2015\ri175\fi-1900\tx2015\plain\f0\fs20 Chord Organ\tab {\f1 chord#10/ding>>E#6;A2D300S70R250}\par
\pard\sa30\li2015\ri175\fi-1900\tx2015\plain\f0\fs20 Ding Bells\tab {\f1 ding#8H0,12/xpding#8T12;R600}\par
\pard\sa30\li2015\ri175\fi-1900\tx2015\plain\f0\fs20 Wide Chimes\tab {\f1 chimes#6Q-80/chimes#6Q80O40/ding>>#4O90L60;R700}\par
\pard\sa30\li2015\ri175\fi-1900\tx2015\plain\f0\fs20 Tada Collage\tab {\f1 tada@t300Mding@t900Ichimes>#16;R400}\par
\pard\sa30\li2015\ri175\fi-1900\tx2015\plain\f0\fs20 Macro Swell\tab {\f1 chord#8/ding>E#8O120Q40;m1=0m11Z50,300m12Q-100,100}\par
\pard\sb40\sa60\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b Note:} Windows 3.11 does not have most of the Windows XP sounds. A name it does not have plays DING.WAV. See {\uldb The Sounds}{\v sounds}.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Your own presets\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Save a preset with the preset menu of your DAW. The preset keeps the DNA, so it gives the same sound in every project. A preset from BASILISK SYNTH, the Windows XP edition also plays here.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To copy a sound as text\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 1\tab Select the text in the DNA box and press CTRL+C.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 2\tab To use a DNA, paste it into the DNA box and press ENTER.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb The DNA}{\v dna}\par
\page
#{\footnote dna}${\footnote The DNA}+{\footnote main:070}K{\footnote DNA;sound;recipe;Apply;Mutate;Undo;rules;canonical form}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b The DNA\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The whole sound of the instrument is one line of text, the DNA. Windows builds the sound from it with the real menu commands of Sound Recorder, from left to right. Because the order changes the result, there are a great many different sounds.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Example\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 chord#10/ding>>E#6;A2D300S70R250}\par
\pard\sa30\li2015\ri175\fi-1900\tx2015\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 chord#10}\tab {\ul Layer}{\v term_layer} 1: chord.wav on 10 recorders.\par
\pard\sa30\li2015\ri175\fi-1900\tx2015\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 /}\tab Starts the next layer.\par
\pard\sa30\li2015\ri175\fi-1900\tx2015\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 ding>>E#6}\tab Layer 2: ding.wav, Increase Speed two times, Add Echo, on 6 recorders.\par
\pard\sa30\li2015\ri175\fi-1900\tx2015\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 ;A2D300S70R250}\tab {\ul Globals}{\v term_global}: attack 2 ms, decay 300 ms, sustain 70%, release 250 ms.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To change the DNA\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 1\tab Type in the DNA box, then press ENTER. ESC puts back the DNA you had.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 2\tab Or choose a command on the panel. The command is added to the DNA of the selected layer.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 3\tab Windows builds the new sound. Your DAW gets the new DNA.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b Undo} goes back to the DNA before the last change. {\b Clear} removes the steps of the selected layer.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Rules\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab A DNA has no spaces and is at most 1024 characters.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Up to 8 layers, and up to 64 steps in each layer.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab A step that would make a sound longer than 30 seconds is skipped.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Unknown characters are skipped. A number out of range is set to the nearest limit.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab An unknown sound name makes that layer silent.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab The plugin keeps the DNA in a tidy form, the {\ul canonical form}{\v term_canonical}.\par
