#{\footnote layers}${\footnote Layers}+{\footnote main:090}K{\footnote layers;Add Layer;Remove Layer;recorders;pool;offset;pan;level;O;Q;L;#}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Layers\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A sound can have up to 8 {\ul layers}{\v term_layer}. Each layer has its own source sound, its own steps and its own {\ul pool}{\v term_pool} of recorders. Every note plays all the layers at once. Layers are separated by {\f1 /} in the DNA.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To add or remove a layer\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Use the Layers buttons on the panel. The header shows "Layer 1 of 2" and so on; < and > select a layer.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Layer genes\par
\pard\sa30\li1215\ri175\fi-1100\tx1215\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 #}{\i N}\tab Recorders for this layer, 1 to 16.\par
\pard\sa30\li1215\ri175\fi-1100\tx1215\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 O}{\i N}\tab {\ul Offset}{\v term_offset}: the layer starts N ms after the note (0 to 4000).\par
\pard\sa30\li1215\ri175\fi-1100\tx1215\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 Q}{\i N}\tab {\ul Pan}{\v term_pan}: -100 is left, 0 is the middle, 100 is right.\par
\pard\sa30\li1215\ri175\fi-1100\tx1215\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 L}{\i N}\tab Level in percent (0 to 400).\par
\pard\sa30\li1215\ri175\fi-1100\tx1215\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 F}\tab Fixed pitch: the layer ignores the note and plays at the sound's own speed. Good for drums.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Offset, pan and level are applied as the sound card's volume while the note plays. They never change the sound that Sound Recorder made.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b How recorders are shared\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 There are 16 recorders. Layers with {\f1 #} get their number first, in order. Layers without {\f1 #} share what is left equally. Every layer gets at least one.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Example\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 chimes#6Q-80/chimes#6Q80O40/ding>>#4O90L60} is chimes on the left, chimes on the right 40 ms later, and a quiet high ding 90 ms after the note.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb Envelopes}{\v envelopes}\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb Harmony, Unison and Transpose}{\v voices}\par
\page
#{\footnote envelopes}${\footnote Envelopes}+{\footnote main:100}K{\footnote envelope;ADSR;attack;decay;sustain;release;drawn envelope;Ja;Jp;breakpoints;pan envelope}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Envelopes\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 An {\ul envelope}{\v term_envelope} shapes the volume of each note over time.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Attack, decay, sustain and release\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The globals {\f1 A}, {\f1 D}, {\f1 S} and {\f1 R} are one envelope for the whole sound. Attack, decay and release are in milliseconds; sustain is a level in percent. You can also set them from your DAW.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Drawn envelopes\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A layer can have its own envelope, drawn as up to 8 {\ul breakpoints}{\v term_breakpoint}.\par
\pard\sa30\li1615\ri175\fi-1500\tx1615\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 Ja}{\i t,l.t,l...}\tab Volume envelope: time in ms after the layer starts, and level 0 to 100.\par
\pard\sa30\li1615\ri175\fi-1500\tx1615\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 Jp}{\i t,l.t,l...}\tab Pan envelope: time in ms, and pan -100 to 100. It adds to the layer's pan.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Between points the level moves in a straight line. The last level holds while the key is down; then the release fades it out. A layer with {\f1 Ja} ignores the global attack, decay and sustain.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Example: {\f1 Ja0,0.30,100.400,60} rises to full in 30 ms, then falls to 60% by 400 ms.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To draw an envelope\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab In the header, {\b Env} shows the selected layer's points. Click < or > to pick a point, + to add one and - to delete one. Then set the point's time and level.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab {\b Envelope}, a seventeenth Sound Recorder, draws the curve in its green wave display.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb Layers}{\v layers}\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb Macros and the Mod Wheel}{\v macros}\par
\page
#{\footnote voices}${\footnote Harmony, Unison and Transpose}+{\footnote main:110}K{\footnote harmony;chords;unison;detune;transpose;octave;voices;H;U;T}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Harmony, Unison and Transpose\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 These genes say how a layer's recorders play each note.\par
\pard\sa30\li1515\ri175\fi-1400\tx1515\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 T}{\i N}\tab {\ul Transpose}{\v term_transpose} the layer by N semitones (-48 to 48).\par
\pard\sa30\li1515\ri175\fi-1400\tx1515\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 H}{\i a,b,...}\tab {\ul Harmony}{\v term_harmony}: each note plays one voice per interval, in semitones. Up to 8.\par
\pard\sa30\li1515\ri175\fi-1400\tx1515\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 U}{\i N}{\f1 ~}{\i C}\tab {\ul Unison}{\v term_unison}: each voice becomes N voices (1 to 8), spread over C {\ul cents}{\v term_cents} (0 to 100).\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Harmony examples\par
\pard\sa30\li1515\ri175\fi-1400\tx1515\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 H0,12}\tab The note and an octave up.\par
\pard\sa30\li1515\ri175\fi-1400\tx1515\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 H0,4,7}\tab A major triad.\par
\pard\sa30\li1515\ri175\fi-1400\tx1515\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 H0,3,7}\tab A minor triad.\par
\pard\sa30\li1515\ri175\fi-1400\tx1515\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 H0,12,-12}\tab Octaves both ways.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The panel has Harmony, Voices, Transpose, Unison and Level controls for the selected layer.\par
\pard\sb40\sa60\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b Tip:} Harmony and unison use more recorders for each note. Give the layer more recorders with {\f1 #} if notes cut each other off.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb Layers}{\v layers}\par
\page
#{\footnote macros}${\footnote Macros and the Mod Wheel}+{\footnote main:120}K{\footnote macros;Macro 1;Macro 2;Macro 3;Macro 4;mod wheel;automation;m}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Macros and the Mod Wheel\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A sound has four {\ul macros}{\v term_macro}. Each macro is a knob for your DAW (Macro 1 to Macro 4) that moves one or more genes of the sound at once. The DNA says what each macro does.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b In the DNA\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Macros are globals, after the {\f1 ;}.\par
\pard\sa30\li2115\ri175\fi-2000\tx2115\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 m}{\i K}{\f1 =}{\i V}\tab Macro K (1 to 4) is at V percent.\par
\pard\sa30\li2115\ri175\fi-2000\tx2115\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 m}{\i K L P min}{\f1 ,}{\i max}\tab Macro K moves gene P of layer L from min (at 0%) to max (at 100%). Layer 0 means every layer.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Genes a macro can move\par
\pard\sa30\li2115\ri175\fi-2000\tx2115\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 O}\tab Offset, in ms.\par
\pard\sa30\li2115\ri175\fi-2000\tx2115\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 Q}\tab Pan.\par
\pard\sa30\li2115\ri175\fi-2000\tx2115\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 L}\tab Level, in percent.\par
\pard\sa30\li2115\ri175\fi-2000\tx2115\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 T}\tab Transpose, in semitones.\par
\pard\sa30\li2115\ri175\fi-2000\tx2115\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 C}\tab Unison spread, in cents.\par
\pard\sa30\li2115\ri175\fi-2000\tx2115\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 Z}\tab Envelope time, in percent (100 is as drawn).\par
\pard\sa30\li2115\ri175\fi-2000\tx2115\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 N}\tab How many times the layer's last step repeats (0 to 8). Windows rebuilds the layer, so it takes a moment.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Example: {\f1 ;m1=0m11Z50,300m12Q-100,100} - Macro 1 slows layer 1's envelope and swings layer 2 from left to right.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b The mod wheel\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The mod wheel adds to Macro 1. With Macro 1 at 0, the wheel moves it from 0 to 100%. A sound that maps nothing to Macro 1 does not change with the wheel.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The Macro 1 to 4 sliders on the panel move the same macros.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb The Keyboard, Bend and Mod Wheel}{\v keyboard}\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb DNA Reference}{\v dna_ref}\par
\page
#{\footnote collages}${\footnote Collages}+{\footnote main:130}K{\footnote collage;Insert File;Mix with File;position;@t;cut-up}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Collages\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A {\ul collage}{\v term_collage} is a sound made of other sounds. It uses the position and the Insert File and Mix with File commands.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Example\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 tada@t300Mding@t900Ichimes>}\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 1\tab Load tada.wav.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 2\tab Move to 300 ms and mix in ding.wav.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 3\tab Move to 900 ms and insert chimes.wav.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 4\tab Increase Speed: the whole collage plays twice as fast.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Any name from {\uldb The Sounds}{\v sounds} can be inserted or mixed, library sounds too.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb Effects and Edits}{\v effects}\par
\page
#{\footnote sounds}${\footnote The Sounds}+{\footnote main:140}K{\footnote sounds;source sounds;Media folder;library;library disc;names of sounds;WAV files}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b The Sounds\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Each layer starts from a sound. C:\\WINDOWS has six:\par
\pard\sa30\li1415\ri175\fi-1300\tx1415\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 chimes}\tab CHIMES.WAV\par
\pard\sa30\li1415\ri175\fi-1300\tx1415\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 chord}\tab CHORD.WAV (from Windows 3.1)\par
\pard\sa30\li1415\ri175\fi-1300\tx1415\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 ding}\tab DING.WAV\par
\pard\sa30\li1415\ri175\fi-1300\tx1415\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 ringin}\tab RINGIN.WAV\par
\pard\sa30\li1415\ri175\fi-1300\tx1415\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 ringout}\tab RINGOUT.WAV\par
\pard\sa30\li1415\ri175\fi-1300\tx1415\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 tada}\tab TADA.WAV (from Windows 3.1)\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Every other Windows XP name in a DNA plays DING.WAV here, so a sound from BASILISK SYNTH, the Windows XP edition still plays.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b The library disc\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Drive L: is the library disc: 249 public domain sounds (instruments, glass and bells, nature), at 22050 Hz so Sound Recorder 3.1 opens them. Use their names in a DNA like the ones above, for example {\f1 kalimba_c4} or {\f1 glass_ping}.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 To choose a sound for the selected layer, pick it from the Sound list in the header.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b The root note\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A sound plays at its own speed on the {\ul root note}{\v term_root}, middle C (60), unless the DNA sets another with {\f1 K}.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb Collages}{\v collages}\par
\page
#{\footnote minimize}${\footnote Minimizing the Synth to Use Windows}+{\footnote main:150}K{\footnote minimize;restore;desktop;Program Manager;using Windows;help, opening;F1}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Minimizing the Synth to Use Windows\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The synth window covers the whole screen of Windows 3.11. You can minimize it and use Windows as usual: Program Manager, File Manager, Notepad, Paintbrush, or this Help. The instrument keeps playing while it is minimized.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To minimize the synth\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Click the Minimize button at the right end of the synth's title bar.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The synth becomes the BASILISK 16 icon at the bottom of the screen.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To restore the synth\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Double-click the BASILISK 16 icon.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b To open this Help\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab In the synth, press F1.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab In Program Manager, open the BASILISK 16 group and double-click {\b BASILISK 16 Help}.\par
\pard\sb40\sa60\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\b Note:} The system sounds of this Windows are turned off, so they never play into your music. When you remove the plugin, this Windows stops, and changes to its files are not kept.\par
\page
#{\footnote menus}${\footnote The Panel}+{\footnote main:160}K{\footnote menus;panels;DNA box;Library;Layers;Macros;Clock;Preset;Sound}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b The Panel\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b The header\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Preset\tab Every factory preset. < and > step through them.\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Sound\tab Every sound: C:\\WINDOWS's six and the library's 249.\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Pan, Offset\tab The selected layer's pan and offset.\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Env\tab The selected layer's drawn envelope. See {\uldb Envelopes}{\v envelopes}.\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Envelope\tab A seventeenth Sound Recorder that draws the envelope.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b The panel\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Sound\tab chimes, ding, ringin, ringout.\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Effects\tab Sound Recorder's commands, added to the DNA.\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Undo, Clear\tab Undo the last change; clear the layer's steps.\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Layers, Harmony\tab Select, add and remove layers; choose a harmony.\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Voices, Transpose, Unison, Level\tab The selected layer's recorders and voice genes.\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Attack, Decay, Sustain, Release\tab The envelope.\par
\pard\sa30\li3015\ri175\fi-2900\tx3015\plain\f0\fs20 Macro 1-4\tab The four macros.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Clock, at the top right, is Windows' own Clock.\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Each change becomes the plugin's sound, and your project saves it.\par
\page
#{\footnote dna_ref}${\footnote DNA Reference}+{\footnote main:170}K{\footnote DNA;reference;steps;genes;globals;syntax;attack;decay;sustain;release;velocity;bend range;root note;gain}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b DNA Reference\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A DNA is: {\i layer} {\f1 /} {\i layer} {\f1 /} ... {\f1 ;} {\i globals}. A layer is a sound name, then steps and genes in any order.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Steps (run in order)\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 +} {\f1 -}\tab Increase / Decrease Volume\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 >} {\f1 <}\tab Increase / Decrease Speed\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 E} {\f1 R}\tab Add Echo, Reverse\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 @}{\i NN} {\f1 @t}{\i N}\tab Move the position to NN% / N ms\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 [} {\f1 ]}\tab Delete Before / After Current Position\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 C} {\f1 P} {\f1 X}\tab Copy, Paste Insert, Paste Mix\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 I}{\i name} {\f1 M}{\i name}\tab Insert File, Mix with File\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Layer genes (the last one of each kind wins)\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 #}{\i N}\tab Recorders, 1 to 16\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 T}{\i N}\tab Transpose, -48 to 48 semitones\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 H}{\i a,b,...}\tab Harmony intervals, up to 8\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 U}{\i N}{\f1 ~}{\i C}\tab Unison: N voices (1 to 8) over C cents (0 to 100)\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 F}\tab Fixed pitch\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 L}{\i N}\tab Level, 0 to 400%\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 O}{\i N}\tab Offset, 0 to 4000 ms\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 Q}{\i N}\tab Pan, -100 to 100\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 Ja}{\i t,l.t,l...}\tab Volume envelope, up to 8 points\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 Jp}{\i t,l.t,l...}\tab Pan envelope, up to 8 points\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Globals (after the semicolon)\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 A}{\i N} {\f1 D}{\i N}\tab Attack, decay in ms (default 0)\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 S}{\i N}\tab Sustain in % (default 100)\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 R}{\i N}\tab Release in ms (default 120)\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 B}{\i N}\tab Pitch bend range in semitones (default 2)\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 V}{\i N}\tab Velocity sensitivity in % (default 100; 0 is every note at full volume)\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 K}{\i N}\tab Root note (default 60, middle C)\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 G}{\i N}\tab Output gain in dB (default 0)\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 m}{\i K}{\f1 =}{\i V}\tab Macro K at V%\par
\pard\sa30\li1815\ri175\fi-1700\tx1815\plain\f0\fs20 {\f1 m}{\i K L P min,max}\tab Macro K moves gene P (O Q L T C Z N) of layer L\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Pitch\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A note plays at the sound's own speed times 2 to the power of (note - K + T + harmony + bend) / 12, plus the unison detune. Speeds outside 2,000 to 192,000 Hz are held at the limit.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b See Also\par
\pard\sa30\li395\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 {\uldb The DNA}{\v dna}\par
