#{\footnote trouble}${\footnote Troubleshooting}+{\footnote main:190}K{\footnote troubleshooting;problems;no sound;silent;late notes;latency;clicks;Windows looks wrong}
\pard\keepn\sb120\sa120\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs24\b Troubleshooting\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b There is no sound\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Wait until the recorders have lined up. Notes sound as soon as Windows is ready.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Check that the DNA names a sound that exists. An unknown name makes its layer silent.\par
\pard\sa40\li395\ri175\fi-275\tx395\plain\f0\fs20 \'b7\tab Check that the layer's level ({\f1 L}) and the Gain are not at the bottom.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Notes come late when I play live\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The plugin has a fixed {\ul latency}{\v term_latency} of 60 ms. Your DAW makes up for it on playback, but you hear it when you play live.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b A note cuts off another note\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Each layer has a limited number of recorders. When they are all busy, the oldest note stops. Give the layer more recorders with {\f1 #}, or use less harmony or unison.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b A step did nothing\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Sound Recorder skipped it. A step that would make the sound longer than 30 seconds is skipped, and so is a step Sound Recorder refuses, like Delete Before at position 0.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b The new sound did not play right away\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Windows builds a new sound with Sound Recorder's commands, one recorder at a time. A long DNA takes longer.\par
\pard\sb115\sa25\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b Something in Windows looks wrong\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Remove the plugin and add it again. Windows starts fresh each time; nothing you change there is kept.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_breakpoint}${\footnote breakpoint}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b breakpoint\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A point of a drawn envelope: a time in milliseconds and a level. The envelope moves in a straight line from one point to the next.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_canonical}${\footnote canonical form}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b canonical form\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The tidy form of a DNA that the plugin keeps: unknown characters removed and numbers written plainly. Presets store it.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_cents}${\footnote cents}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b cents\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A hundredth of a semitone. Unison spreads its voices over a few cents so they shimmer.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_collage}${\footnote collage}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b collage\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A sound made from other sounds with Insert File and Mix with File at chosen positions.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_daw}${\footnote DAW}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b DAW\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Digital audio workstation: the music program that hosts the plugin, such as Ableton Live, Cubase, Logic, REAPER or Bitwig.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_dna}${\footnote DNA}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b DNA\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 One line of text that is the whole sound: its layers, the Sound Recorder commands that build each one, and the settings for playing it.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_envelope}${\footnote envelope}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b envelope\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 How the volume (or the pan) of a note changes over time, from the moment it starts until it fades out.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_gene}${\footnote gene}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b gene\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A letter and number in a layer that says how the layer plays, such as #8 (eight recorders) or Q-50 (pan left). Genes do not change the sound itself.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_global}${\footnote global}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b global\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A setting for the whole sound, written after the semicolon: attack, decay, sustain, release, bend range, velocity, root note, gain and macros.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_harmony}${\footnote harmony}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b harmony\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Extra voices at fixed intervals above or below each note, such as an octave or a triad.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_latency}${\footnote latency}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b latency\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The delay between a note and its sound. The plugin reports it to the DAW, which makes up for it on playback.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_layer}${\footnote layer}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b layer\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 One part of a sound: a source sound, its steps and its genes, on its own recorders. A note plays every layer at once.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_library}${\footnote library disc}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b library disc\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A disc of 249 public domain sounds that the plugin puts in the machine, usable by name in a DNA.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_macro}${\footnote macro}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b macro\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 One of four knobs (Macro 1 to 4) that move several genes of a sound at once. Your DAW can automate them.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_modwheel}${\footnote mod wheel}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b mod wheel\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The modulation wheel of a MIDI keyboard (controller 1). Here it adds to Macro 1.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_offset}${\footnote offset}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b offset\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 How long after the note a layer starts, in milliseconds.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_pan}${\footnote pan}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b pan\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Where a sound sits between the left and right speakers.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_bend}${\footnote pitch bend}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b pitch bend\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The pitch wheel of a MIDI keyboard. It bends notes up or down by the bend range (B). The bend is set when each note starts.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_pool}${\footnote pool}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b pool\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The recorders that belong to one layer. A note takes a free recorder from each layer's pool.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_preset}${\footnote preset}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b preset\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 A saved sound. A preset is just its DNA.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_recorder}${\footnote recorder}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b recorder\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 One of the sixteen Sound Recorders that play the notes. Each is a strip of the synth.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_root}${\footnote root note}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b root note\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The note that plays a sound at its own speed. Higher notes play it faster, lower notes slower. Middle C (60) unless the DNA says K.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_semitone}${\footnote semitone}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b semitone\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 The step from one key to the next on a piano keyboard. Twelve make an octave.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_step}${\footnote step}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b step\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 One Sound Recorder command in a layer, such as > (Increase Speed) or E (Add Echo). Steps change the sound itself, in order.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_transpose}${\footnote transpose}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b transpose\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 To move notes up or down by a number of semitones.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_unison}${\footnote unison}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b unison\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 Several copies of each voice, slightly out of tune with each other, for a fuller sound.\par
\page
#{\footnote term_velocity}${\footnote velocity}
\pard\sb60\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20\b velocity\par
\pard\sa60\li115\ri175\plain\f0\fs20 How hard a key is played (1 to 127). The V setting says how much it changes the volume.\par
}
